  "summary": "Sonic's Sega CD adventure turns speed into a way of travelling through time, with several versions of each stage to explore.",
  "body": [
    "Sonic CD is Sega’s side-scrolling platform adventure for the Sega CD, first released in Japan on 23 September 1993. Sonic travels to Little Planet, where Doctor Robotnik is seeking the Time Stones. Rings, rolling attacks and loops provide familiar ways to move and survive, while Amy Rose and Metal Sonic make their series debuts. Naoto Ohshima’s team built a separate adventure around the CD hardware, giving the journey animated opening and closing sequences and a world whose appearance changes with the period Sonic visits.",
    "A sign marked Past or Future begins the possibility of time travel. Sonic must then maintain enough speed to make the transition, so finding a suitable stretch of ground becomes part of exploring the stage. The same area can contain different platforms, machines and vegetation in another period. Travelling into the past also allows Sonic to destroy the machinery producing Robotnik’s enemies. That action changes the future of the zone, making a good outcome something the player can bring about inside the level.",
    "The changes in time are audible as well as visible. Naofumi Hataya and Masafumi Ogata composed the original Japanese and European soundtrack, while the North American edition received music by Spencer Nilsen, David Young and Sterling Crew. Separate arrangements accompany different periods of a zone. Special stages offer another route to a good future: Sonic pursues UFOs across a timed, perspective-view course to earn Time Stones. Collecting all seven changes the outcome, giving exploration and these separate challenges a shared purpose in the adventure."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Little Planet",
    "Running into another time",
    "Two sounds for one adventure"
  ],
